this plans to bring more affordable  housing to the valley now on hold  neighbors are really divided over a  project to build a low-income community  near north decatur in the 215. madison  kimbrough heard from both sides after  sitting in on tonight's planning  commission meeting  the city asked the public their thoughts  and they responded the majority opposed  to having this low-income multi-family  development in their neighborhood as  they say the increase of crime and  congestion is of top concern the  276-unit facility would be located on  rome and decatur the nearly 9.5 acres of  land was provided to the city of las  vegas by the bureau of land management  marked solely to be used for affordable  housing and through a bidding process a  company called ovation won the bid thus  paying a dollar for legality purposes  and taking over the cost of construction  with a plan to manage the property for  15 years the majority of attendees at  city hall tonight opposed to the idea  while some of the few there believe a  lot of good can come from it  our number one concern is the high  density component and it's not the  income component it's the amount of  pressure that will be put in that exact  location  by traffic for schooling for  emergency responses it does improve the  neighborhood it increases property  values it actually lowers crime they've  got a lot of fears regarding affordable  housing it's people just like you and me  now in the end the city commissioners  voted on a 60-day hold and they will  revisit the discussion on september 13th  and while the environment tonight was  tense at times the majority were pleased  that the city decided to give it more  time before making a final decision in  the newsroom madison kimbrough live  local now
 

